// heartsim patient case: syncope and progressive conduction disease. Original text by the heartsim
// authors, MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/case.rs.
Case(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "case_syncope_heart_block",
    title: "Collapse in the supermarket",
    summary: "An older woman faints twice in a week. Follow her ECG from a quiet baseline to the rhythm that explains the collapses, and decide what to do.",
    patient: "74-year-old woman, two episodes of sudden loss of consciousness in a week, no chest pain, no warning, on no heart-rate-lowering drugs.",
    references: [
        "Kusumoto FM et al. 2018 ACC/AHA/HRS guideline on the evaluation and management of patients with bradycardia and cardiac conduction delay. Circulation 2019;140:e382-e482.",
        "Glikson M et al. 2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiac pacing and cardiac resynchronization therapy. Eur Heart J 2021;42:3427-3520.",
        "Brignole M et al. 2018 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of syncope. Eur Heart J 2018;39:1883-1948.",
        "Panchal AR et al. Part 3: Adult basic and advanced life support: 2020 American Heart Association Guidelines for CPR and ECC. Circulation 2020;142:S366-S468.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: bifascicular block, Mobitz II, third-degree AV block, pacemaker rhythms. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
    ],
    stages: [
        Stage(
            title: "The first ECG",
            markdown: r#"
**Mrs Rossi** is brought to the emergency department after fainting in a supermarket. She felt
nothing before she fell, and recovered within a minute. Her examination is normal and her blood
pressure is 135/80 mmHg.

Her 12-lead ECG shows sinus rhythm at 72 bpm, a PR interval of 190 ms, a **wide QRS** with an rSR' in
V1, and a frontal axis of about −60°.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("bifascicular")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What conduction abnormality does the ECG show?",
                    choices: [
                        "Right bundle-branch block with left anterior fascicular block (bifascicular block)",
                        "Left bundle-branch block",
                        "First-degree AV block only",
                        "A normal ECG for her age",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A wide QRS with an rSR' in V1 is right bundle-branch block. The axis of −60° adds left anterior fascicular block. Two of the three divisions of the conduction system are blocked.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why does unexplained syncope make this ECG worrying?",
                    choices: [
                        "The last fascicle may fail intermittently and cause complete heart block",
                        "RBBB always causes syncope",
                        "It proves a pulmonary embolism",
                        "It shows a long QT interval",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "With only the left posterior fascicle conducting, a transient failure produces complete AV block with a pause, which can cause sudden syncope. Syncope with bifascicular block is a reason for urgent investigation, usually with a pacemaker or prolonged monitoring.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Monitoring on the ward",
            markdown: r#"
She is admitted to a monitored bed. During the night the nurse calls you: the monitor shows
**dropped beats**. The rhythm strip shows regular P waves at 80 bpm; **three of every four P waves are
followed by a wide QRS**, and the PR interval of the conducted beats is the **same each time**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_2nd_mobitz2_4to3")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What is the rhythm?",
                    choices: [
                        "Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II",
                        "Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach)",
                        "Sinoatrial exit block",
                        "Premature atrial contractions",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A constant PR interval in the conducted beats, followed by a sudden dropped QRS, is Mobitz II. In Mobitz I the PR would lengthen before each drop.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Atropine is unlikely to help here. Why?",
                    choices: [
                        "The block is below the AV node, and atropine acts on the node",
                        "Atropine only works in the ventricles",
                        "She is too old for atropine",
                        "Atropine makes the QRS narrow",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "Atropine removes vagal tone from the sinus and AV nodes. Block in the His-Purkinje system is not under vagal control, and a faster atrial rate can even worsen it by presenting more impulses to the block.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "She collapses on the monitor",
            markdown: r#"
Ten minutes later the alarm sounds. Mrs Rossi is **pale and unresponsive but has a faint pulse**. The
monitor shows P waves at 80 bpm that **no longer conduct at all**, and slow, **wide** QRS complexes at
35 bpm.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_3rd_ventricular")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What is the rhythm now?",
                    choices: [
                        "Complete heart block with a ventricular escape rhythm",
                        "Ventricular tachycardia",
                        "Sinus bradycardia",
                        "Atrial fibrillation",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "P waves and QRS complexes beat independently (AV dissociation), and the QRS complexes are wide and slow. The ventricles are being driven by an escape focus below the block.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "What is the right immediate treatment for this unstable patient?",
                    choices: [
                        "Transcutaneous (or transvenous) pacing, with a chronotropic infusion as a bridge",
                        "Adenosine 6 mg",
                        "Amiodarone",
                        "Synchronised cardioversion at 100 J",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A bradycardic patient who is unstable needs a faster rate. Pacing is the reliable treatment when the block is infranodal; an infusion of adrenaline or dopamine can bridge until the pacemaker is ready. Shocks and adenosine are for tachycardias.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "After the pacemaker",
            markdown: r#"
A dual-chamber pacemaker is implanted the next day. Her ECG shows a **thin spike before every wide QRS**,
and the **ventricular rate follows the sinus P waves** at 75 bpm.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_ddd")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which pacing mode keeps the ventricles in step with the sinus node here?",
                    choices: [
                        "DDD, which senses the atrium and paces the ventricle after an AV delay",
                        "VVI, which paces the ventricle at a fixed rate",
                        "AOO, which paces the atrium without sensing",
                        "VOO, which paces the ventricle without sensing",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "In DDD mode the pacemaker tracks each sensed P wave and paces the ventricle after the programmed AV delay, so the heart rate follows the sinus rate and AV synchrony is kept. VVI ignores the atria.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why is the QRS wide after pacing?",
                    choices: [
                        "The lead in the right ventricle starts activation in muscle, not in the Purkinje network",
                        "The pacemaker widens the P wave",
                        "The block has become worse",
                        "The sinus node is not working",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A paced beat starts at the tip of the lead and spreads cell to cell through the ventricular muscle, which takes longer than the normal His-Purkinje route. That is why paced QRS complexes have a left bundle-branch block shape.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
